= Section A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= 18
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**A is poly(norbornene); B is the corresponding sequential <block copolymer> with a methyl-ester-substituted second block.** The process is <ring-opening metathesis polymerization>. Each strained bicyclic <alkene> becomes a cyclopentane unit whose two backbone attachment positions are joined to neighbouring units through $\mathrm{-CH=CH-}$ links. The second block retains its $\mathrm{CO_2Me}$ substituent on the cyclopentane framework. Thus a useful connectivity representation is $\mathrm{end-[CH=CH-C_5H_8]_{n}-[CH=CH-C_5H_7(CO_2Me)]_{m}-end}$, with the cyclopentane attachment positions at 1 and 3. This representation specifies connectivity rather than a single backbone <alkene> geometry.

The ruthenium <alkylidene> reacts with a <monomer> <alkene> through a <metallacyclobutane>, then fragments with the other pair of bonds broken. A new ruthenium <alkylidene> remains at the growing end, allowing propagation and then addition of the second <monomer>. Relief of the bicyclic <monomer>'s <ring strain> supplies the principal enthalpic driving force; the loss of <monomer> translational <entropy> opposes <polymerization>. Sequential addition yields blocks only if the first population of active ends survives until the second addition.
\Image[paper-2-polymer-connectivity.png]
{title=Original polymer connectivity fragments and the reductive ozonolysis product}

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

With one growing chain per ruthenium initiator and negligible <chain transfer>, consumption of <monomer> gives
$$
\boxed{DP_n=\frac{[M]_0}{[I]_0}x=50(0.98)=49.}
$$
The conversion factor applies to incorporated <monomer> units, not the number of active chains. Slow initiation or inactive initiator would invalidate this ideal <living polymerization> estimate.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A sufficiently phase-separated <block copolymer> can show **two <glass transitions>, associated approximately with its two microdomains**. Simultaneous feeding instead tends to distribute the <monomers> along each chain, giving a <statistical copolymer> and usually one composition-dependent <glass transition>. Different <monomer> reactivities could produce a gradient rather than a perfectly random sequence.

For a homogeneous mixture an approximate interpolation is the <Fox equation>, $T_g^{-1}=w_1/T_{g1}+w_2/T_{g2}$, with temperatures in kelvin. Two transitions are not guaranteed merely by block architecture: short blocks, miscibility or insufficient experimental resolution can give one broad transition. The contrast is between domain-specific segmental environments and a more uniform mixed environment.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**C has a saturated backbone and pendant $\mathrm{CH_2OH}$ groups in its second block.** <Hydrogenation> converts every backbone $\mathrm{CH=CH}$ to $\mathrm{CH_2CH_2}$; <lithium aluminium hydride> then reduces each $\mathrm{CO_2Me}$ to $\mathrm{CH_2OH}$. The resulting connectivity is $\mathrm{[CH_2CH_2-C_5H_8]_{50}-[CH_2CH_2-C_5H_7(CH_2OH)]_{75}}$.

<Hydrogenation> removes local double-bond rigidity and commonly lowers the segmental barrier to motion, favouring a lower <glass transition temperature>. <Ester> reduction changes polarity and side-group volume, while the new hydroxyl groups introduce <hydrogen bonding>, which can raise the hydroxyl-rich block's <glass transition temperature> and strengthen segregation. These competing effects preclude a numerical transition shift or an unconditional direction for both transitions from structure alone.

The repeat-unit formulae after both operations are $\mathrm{C_7H_{12}}$ and $\mathrm{C_8H_{14}O}$. Taking standard atomic masses,
$$
\boxed{M_{n,\mathrm{repeat}}=50(96.173)+75(126.199)=1.4274\times10^4\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}.}
$$
This is **approximately 14.27 kg mol$^{-1}$ plus the net end-group mass**. The termination chemistry is unspecified, so a more precise whole-chain mass is not determined; the methyl group lost during <ester> reduction must not remain in the second repeat's mass.

= e
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Pendant <alcohols> can form **reversible <borate> bridges**, schematically $\mathrm{P-O-B(OH)-O-P'}$ and $\mathrm{B(O-P)_3}$, where P and P′ belong to different chains. Tetrahedral <borate> species are also possible depending on pH and water content. A bridge must connect different network strands to act as a <cross-link>; an intrachain loop does not contribute equivalently to elasticity.

Between <cross-links>, stretching reduces the number of accessible chain conformations. A simple incompressible <rubber elasticity> model gives the nominal tensile stress
$$
\boxed{\sigma_{\rm nominal}=G(\lambda-\lambda^{-2}),\qquad G\simeq\nu_e k_BT,}
$$
where $\lambda$ is the extension ratio and $\nu_e$ the density of elastically active strands. The initial response is soft; stronger extension gives increasing stress and ultimately finite-chain extensibility. Reversible <borate> exchange permits <stress relaxation>, rate dependence and a loading–unloading <hysteresis> loop, rather than permanent ideal elasticity at arbitrarily long times.

\Image[paper-2-polymer-responses.png]
{title=Original schematic rubber stress, ester-block cleavage and amphiphilic assembly}

= f
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

**Reductive <ozonolysis> cuts the unsaturated backbone into cyclopentane-1,3-dicarbaldehyde units**, apart from end fragments. Both <alkene> carbons carry a hydrogen, so the carbonyl products are <aldehydes> rather than <ketones>. Ring substituent <stereochemistry> is inherited from the <polymer> repeat; backbone E/Z geometry does not survive cleavage.

Ozone adds across a backbone <alkene> by <cycloaddition>, giving a primary ozonide. Its fragmentation produces a <carbonyl group> and a <carbonyl oxide>, which recombine to a secondary ozonide. <Dimethyl sulfide> reduces the peroxide-containing intermediate, forming dimethyl sulfoxide and the two carbonyl termini. Repetition at every backbone double bond liberates the dialdehyde. An oxidizing work-up would instead risk oxidation to carboxylic acids, so the specified reductive work-up is essential.

= 19
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $R=\mathrm{CH_2CH(Ph)-O-TEMPO}$, so B is $\mathrm{HO-R}$. Its hydroxyl group initiates <ring-opening polymerization> of lactide, giving
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{C=H-[OCH(Me)CO]_{2n}-O-CH_2CH(Ph)-O-TEMPO}.}
$$
Each lactide contributes two lactyl repeat units. Dry <monomer>, exclusion of water and an alcohol-compatible catalyst such as tin(II) 2-ethylhexanoate under heating provide a practical route. <Alcohol>/metal-alkoxide exchange followed by lactide coordination and acyl–oxygen cleavage extends the <ester> chain while retaining a terminal hydroxyl group.

Heating C under oxygen-free conditions homolytically cleaves its benzylic C–O bond to the nitroxide, releasing a benzylic radical and TEMPO. The radical adds styrene repeatedly; reversible trapping by TEMPO controls the active radical concentration. D is therefore a **poly(lactide)–<polystyrene> <block copolymer>** with junction $\mathrm{PLA-OCH_2CH(Ph)-[CH_2CH(Ph)]_m-O-TEMPO}$ and the hydroxyl end of PLA retained. This is <nitroxide-mediated polymerization>; a temperature around 120–130 °C is a representative condition for a TEMPO/styrene system, rather than an exact universal optimum.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**Styrene, benzoyl peroxide and TEMPO** provide the required radical addition–trapping sequence. Heating the peroxide breaks its O–O bond into benzoyloxy radicals. Before decarboxylation, $\mathrm{PhCO_2^{\bullet}}$ can add to styrene's terminal carbon, generating the resonance-stabilized radical $\mathrm{PhCO_2CH_2\dot CHPh}$. Coupling with the oxygen-centred TEMPO radical then gives $\mathrm{PhCO_2CH_2CH(Ph)-O-TEMPO}$, the benzoate-protected alkoxyamine A.

The connectivity distinguishes this route from initiators that leave a carbon-based radical fragment at the chain end. Competition with benzoyloxy-radical decarboxylation affects yield, so this is a plausible preparation rather than a claim of exclusive product formation. Subsequent benzoate cleavage reveals B's hydroxyl group without requiring rupture of its alkoxyamine linkage.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**The ideal number-average molar mass is approximately 25.11 kg mol$^{-1}$.** Quantitative uptake of 100 lactide and 100 styrene molecules per bifunctional initiator gives
$$
\boxed{M_n=M_B+100M_{\rm lactide}+100M_{\rm styrene}\simeq277.41+100(144.13)+100(104.15)=25105.4\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}.}
$$
B has formula $\mathrm{C_{17}H_{27}NO_2}$. Lactide's entire cyclic diester mass is incorporated; multiplying its molar mass by two again would double-count its two lactyl units. The estimate assumes quantitative initiation, conversion, nitroxide recapping and no chain transfer or loss of chain-end fragments.

= d
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Hydroxide attacks a PLA <ester> carbonyl, moving the $\pi$ electrons onto oxygen. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate expels an alkoxide; proton transfer produces an <alcohol> and a carboxylate. The carboxylate stabilizes the hydrolysis products and makes this <saponification> effectively irreversible under the basic conditions. Repetition cleaves the PLA block, including its terminal <ester> linkage to the junction <alcohol>, while the carbon–carbon <polystyrene> backbone remains intact.

**The principal surviving <polymer> is a <polystyrene> chain bearing the junction $\mathrm{HOCH_2CH(Ph)-}$ end and its nitroxide-derived distal end.** In <gel permeation chromatography>, the intact <block copolymer> normally elutes earlier than this smaller surviving <polystyrene> coil. Lactate and short hydrolysis fragments elute later still, provided the column and detector resolve such small solutes. The <polymer> peak therefore shifts to larger elution volume; its height need not remain constant under a mass-sensitive detector. Elution reflects <hydrodynamic volume>, so calibration against <polystyrene> standards does not supply an exact absolute mass for the original mixed block.

= e
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Replacing lactide by ethylene oxide gives
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{HO-[CH_2CH_2O]_{50}-CH_2CH(Ph)-[CH_2CH(Ph)]_{100}-O-TEMPO}.}
$$
The first chain grows by alkoxide attack on ethylene oxide, followed by protonation at work-up; the alkoxyamine subsequently controls styrene <polymerization>. The final material is an <amphiphilic block copolymer>. In water, <polystyrene> avoids solvent exposure while the poly(ethylene oxide) block is hydrated. Above a concentration-dependent assembly threshold, **<micelles> with a <polystyrene> core and hydrated poly(ethylene oxide) corona** are a reasonable morphology. Unimers remain in equilibrium with aggregates. Core–corona assembly lowers unfavourable water contact but costs chain-stretching and translational <entropy>. Block volume fractions, solvent history and concentration can instead favour cylinders or vesicles, so the chemical structure alone does not uniquely establish spherical <micelles>.

= Section B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= 20
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Place the trigonal axis along $z$, the axial ligands on $\pm z$ and the equatorial ligands in the $xy$ plane. **The ordering is $e''(d_{xz},d_{yz})<e'(d_{xy},d_{x^2-y^2})<a_1'(d_{z^2})$.** The $e''$ pair has nodal behaviour along both the equatorial ligand directions and the axial axis, minimizing direct ligand repulsion. The in-plane $e'$ pair points into the equatorial region. The $d_{z^2}$ lobes point at both axial ligands and its torus also samples the equatorial ligands, giving the largest repulsion in the regular point-charge geometry.

Rotation by 120° and reflection in the equatorial plane preserve the geometry; they enforce the indicated doublet degeneracies. The ordering concerns a regular <trigonal bipyramid> with equivalent ligand-field assumptions, not every distorted five-coordinate complex.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For two independent identical ions at sufficiently high temperature, <Curie's law> gives $\chi T=2(N_A\mu_B^2/3k_B)g^2S(S+1)$. In the cgs units used here, $N_A\mu_B^2/3k_B\simeq0.125\ \mathrm{cm^3\,K\,mol^{-1}}$.

**Fe(II), Co(II) and Ni(II) have $S=2$, $3/2$ and 1, respectively.** The corresponding high-spin occupancies, ordered as $(e'',e',a_1')$, are
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{Fe^{II}}:d^6=(3,2,1),\qquad\mathrm{Co^{II}}:d^7=(4,2,1),\qquad\mathrm{Ni^{II}}:d^8=(4,3,1).}
$$
Hund filling puts one electron in each orbital before additional pairing. Spin-only dimer values at $g=2$ would be 6.0, 3.75 and 2.0; the observed larger values anticipate orbital contributions. The <spin> assignments require the chemically appropriate high-spin states, rather than rounding the measured $\chi T$ directly to a spin-only integer.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Using the <spin> values above and accounting for **two metal ions per formula unit**,
$$
\boxed{g_{\rm Fe}=\sqrt{7.4/1.5}=2.22,\quad g_{\rm Co}=\sqrt{5.2/0.9375}=2.36,\quad g_{\rm Ni}=\sqrt{3.4/0.5}=2.61.}
$$
The deviations from 2 indicate <spin-orbit coupling> and incomplete quenching of <orbital angular momentum>. The partly filled $e''$ manifold in Fe and $e'$ manifold in Ni permit orbital degeneracy in the ideal geometry. Co's high-spin orbital occupation is more orbitally balanced, but mixing with excited ligand-field states still produces a substantial effective $g$ shift. These fitted scalar values summarize a thermally averaged response; they do not determine the individual components of an anisotropic <g tensor>.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The radical's magnetic orbital has $\pi$ symmetry. Fe(II)'s partly occupied $e''$ set supplies a matching metal $\pi$ magnetic orbital, permitting electron transfer and **predominantly antiferromagnetic metal–radical exchange**. In Co(II) and Ni(II), that set is filled; their unpaired metal electrons occupy orbitals of different symmetry. Approximate orthogonality suppresses the usual antiferromagnetic kinetic-exchange pathway, favouring **ferromagnetic metal–radical coupling** through the remaining exchange contributions.

This orbital-symmetry argument assumes the intended idealized binding geometry. It predicts exchange signs, not exact magnitudes; distortion and additional orbital mixing can alter the competition.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Adopt $\mathcal H=-2J(\mathbf S_1+\mathbf S_2)\cdot\mathbf s-2J'\mathbf S_1\cdot\mathbf S_2$, with $S_1=S_2=1$ and ligand <spin> $s=1/2$. Let $\mathbf S_{12}=\mathbf S_1+\mathbf S_2$ and $\mathbf S=\mathbf S_{12}+\mathbf s$. The <angular momentum addition> identities give
$$
E=-J\{S(S+1)-S_{12}(S_{12}+1)-3/4\}-J'\{S_{12}(S_{12}+1)-4\}.
$$
The five allowed multiplets are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
S_{12}&S&E\\\hline
0&1/2&4J'\\
1&1/2&2J+2J'\\
1&3/2&-J+2J'\\
2&3/2&3J-2J'\\
2&5/2&-2J-2J'
\end{array}
$$
Their degeneracies $2,2,4,4,6$ sum to 18, matching $3\times3\times2$. **For dominant ferromagnetic $J$, the ground multiplet has $S_{12}=2$, $S=5/2$.** A weak antiferromagnetic $J'$ does not overcome the two strong metal–radical bonds.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A frustrated triangle has two ferromagnetic metal–radical bonds and an antiferromagnetic metal–metal bond. With the Hamiltonian convention above, take $J/k_B=+800$ K and $J'/k_B=-8$ K. In the maximally aligned <ground state> the two metal–radical products are $1\times1/2$ and the metal–metal product is $1\times1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{F_C=-\frac{2(800)(1/2)-8}{2(800)(1/2)+8}=-\frac{792}{808}=-0.9802.}
$$
Here $F_C=-1$ denotes complete satisfaction of all exchange interactions; the small deviation $1+F_C=0.0198$ quantifies the weak frustrated contribution in this normalization. **The numerical answer assumes antiferromagnetic metal–metal exchange.** The printed magnitudes alone do not establish that sign. If the metal–metal exchange were instead ferromagnetic, all three bonds would be satisfied and $F_C=-1$. The ground-state scalar products also follow directly from $S_{12}=2$, $S=5/2$, so a separate classical approximation is unnecessary for this fully polarized multiplet.

= d
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For an axial <spin> Hamiltonian $\mathcal H_D=D S_z^2$ with $D/k_B=-25$ K, the minima are $m_S=\pm5/2$. The highest levels along the reversal ladder are the $m_S=\pm1/2$ doublet. Hence
$$
\boxed{U/k_B=|D|\{(5/2)^2-(1/2)^2\}=25(6)=150\ \mathrm K,}
$$
about 1.25 kJ mol$^{-1}$ or 12.9 meV per molecule. Using $|D|S^2$ would incorrectly put a half-integer <spin> at a nonexistent $m_S=0$ level. Transverse anisotropy and <quantum tunnelling of magnetization> can create faster relaxation pathways, so this axial ladder barrier need not equal an experimentally fitted effective barrier.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**Frequency-dependent ac susceptibility** probes relaxation: a peak in the out-of-phase component occurs approximately when $\omega\tau=1$. Measuring its displacement with frequency determines $\tau(T)$; an activated region may fit $\tau=\tau_0\exp(U_{\rm eff}/k_BT)$. A low-temperature departure can reveal tunnelling or other relaxation channels.

**Low-temperature, sweep-rate-dependent <magnetization> <hysteresis>** tests whether reversal is slow on the measurement timescale. A loop opening below the blocking temperature, variation with sweep rate and possible tunnelling steps provide complementary evidence. Isothermal remanent <magnetization> decay after field removal directly measures the associated relaxation time. A single open loop alone would not distinguish isolated-molecule blocking from collective ordering or impurities.

= 21
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The framework is a **three-dimensional network of corner-connected $\mathrm{MO_6}$ octahedra**, with each apparent corner connection extended through a bridging formate, $\mathrm{M-O-CH-O-M}$. Each metal is coordinated by six oxygen donors from six formates, which link it to six neighbouring metals in the perovskite-like topology. The guanidinium cation occupies the larger cavity and can hydrogen-bond to formate oxygen atoms. Charge balance is $+1+2-3=0$.

\Image[paper-2-formate-magnetism.png]
{title=Original formate-network topology and weak-ferromagnetic magnetization schematic}

= b
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At high temperature, approximately independent paramagnetic ions give the appropriate <Curie constant>. The negative <Weiss temperatures> and the reduction of $\chi T$ on cooling initially indicate **predominantly antiferromagnetic correlations**. The sharp rise near the ordering temperature nevertheless reveals a strongly enhanced low-field magnetic response.

A net moment could arise from a <canted antiferromagnet>, a <ferrimagnet>, or a ferromagnet with competing antiferromagnetic interactions. Differences between <field-cooled magnetization> and <zero-field-cooled magnetization> suggest history-dependent domain selection or slow relaxation, but are not alone proof of a particular microscopic <spin> arrangement. An ordinary compensated antiferromagnet would not account for a large ordering-related ferromagnetic-like low-field contribution without an additional mechanism.

= c
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**Weak <ferromagnetism> from canted antiferromagnetic order is the consistent model.** Importantly, the plot has different <magnetization> scales: the left axis belongs to Mn and Fe; the right belongs to Co and Ni. Approximate low-temperature field-cooled readings are 40, 50, 450 and 390 G cm$^3$ mol$^{-1}$, respectively. Dividing by 5585 gives
$$
\boxed{M_{\rm FC}\simeq(0.0072,\ 0.0090,\ 0.081,\ 0.070)\ \mu_B\text{ per metal}.}
$$
These moments are tiny compared with fully aligned moments of about 5.09, 4.40, 4.25 and 2.47 $\mu_B$, calculated using the Curie-derived $g$ values below. A conventional ferromagnet of the full high-spin ions would have much larger spontaneous <magnetization>; a simple bipartite array of identical metal sites also supplies no obvious unequal sublattice moments for ferrimagnetism. Opposed large sublattice moments with a small transverse uncompensated component reconcile the net response with negative $\Theta$.

If the 10 Oe field has aligned the weak-ferromagnetic domains, $M_{\rm weak}=gS\mu_B\sin\alpha$ estimates sublattice canting angles of roughly 0.08°, 0.12°, 1.1° and 1.6°. These are illustrative, not precision structure determinations: the low field may not fully align the domains, and the readings are graphical. Bulk <magnetization> strongly supports this model but does not uniquely establish every <spin> direction; <magnetic neutron diffraction> would test the microscopic arrangement.

= d
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Use $C=0.125g^2S(S+1)$ for **one** ion per formula unit, with high-spin $S=5/2,2,3/2,1$ for Mn, Fe, Co, Ni. Then
$$
\boxed{g\simeq(2.04,\ 2.20,\ 2.83,\ 2.47).}
$$
High-spin Mn(II), with an approximately orbitally nondegenerate $d^5$ <ground state>, stays closest to the free-electron value. Fe, Co and Ni have stronger orbital admixture, particularly Co in this effective Curie-law comparison. <Spin-orbit coupling> creates <magnetic anisotropy>, which can make reversal of the weak-ferromagnetic domains slower and enhance field-cooled/zero-field-cooled separation. A field-cooled sample selects domains as it orders; a zero-field-cooled sample initially has competing domains and can remain pinned when a small measuring field is applied.

This is a qualitative rationalization, not a strict proportionality between $g-2$ and the <magnetization> separation. The anisotropy tensor, domain-wall pinning and experimental timescale also matter; scalar $g$ values alone do not quantitatively fix the divergence.

= e
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Define $\mathcal H=-2J\sum_{\langle ij\rangle}\mathbf S_i\cdot\mathbf S_j$. Each site has $z=6$ exchange-connected neighbours. In <mean-field theory>, replacing neighbours by their mean <spin> produces the effective field that yields
$$
\Theta=\frac{2zJS(S+1)}{3k_B},\qquad\frac{J}{k_B}=\frac{\Theta}{4S(S+1)}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{J/k_B\simeq(-0.414,\ -0.704,\ -3.55,\ -8.96)\ \mathrm K}
$$
for Mn, Fe, Co and Ni. Negative $J$ means antiferromagnetic exchange in this convention; a Hamiltonian written with $-J$ instead of $-2J$ would have twice these numerical values. The increasingly negative values indicate a growing effective exchange energy, although different orbital occupancy and anisotropy mean that they are not a simple universal function of $d$-electron number.

A three-dimensional six-connected framework is more favourable to mean-field reasoning than isolated chains, but the theory neglects short-range correlations, anisotropic exchange and fluctuations. The ratios $|\Theta|/T_C\simeq1.65,1.69,3.75,2.10$ show substantial departures from the simplest unfrustrated isotropic mean-field relation. The values are therefore **effective high-temperature exchange estimates**, not a complete microscopic fit to the ordered phases.

= f
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

For Co, the full field-polarized ionic value is
$$
\boxed{M_{\rm sat}=N_AgS\mu_B\simeq4.25\ \mu_B\text{ per Co}\simeq2.37\times10^4\ \mathrm{G\,cm^3\,mol^{-1}}.}
$$
This is distinct from the much smaller weak-ferromagnetic domain moment, approximately 450 G cm$^3$ mol$^{-1}$ at low field. A low-temperature isothermal loop should have a **small hysteretic central component superposed on the antiferromagnetic field response**. Reversal changes the selected weak-ferromagnetic domain and produces remanence and coercivity. At larger fields the <magnetization> continues to increase as the opposed sublattice moments rotate; a spin-flop or spin-reorientation feature may occur depending on field direction and anisotropy. Only sufficiently strong fields approach the full ionic saturation value, so the small low-field loop must not be labelled as saturation at 4.25 $\mu_B$.

= g
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

Below the ordering temperature, coherent <spin> correlations produce **magnetic Bragg intensity**. Depending on the magnetic propagation vector, this can appear as additional peaks or as added intensity at positions already occupied by nuclear reflections. A magnetic unit cell larger than the chemical one gives extra reciprocal-space positions; a zero-propagation-vector canted structure need not do so.

Magnetic intensity generally decreases at large scattering vector through the <magnetic form factor> and depends on moment components perpendicular to the scattering vector. Above the transition the long-range magnetic Bragg contribution disappears, though short-range correlations can leave diffuse scattering. The nuclear pattern remains approximately unchanged unless magnetic ordering also drives a structural distortion.

