= Solution
{parent=i}

In <electrophoresis>, an applied <electric field> drives a charged particle and its associated diffuse ionic atmosphere relative to the liquid. Electric forcing is balanced by viscous stresses, giving a steady velocity $\mathbf v=\mu_e\mathbf E$ in the linear regime. The relevant electrokinetic potential is the <zeta potential> at the slipping plane, rather than necessarily the solid surface potential.

For a thin <electric double layer>, the surrounding layer develops an electro-osmotic flow and the <Smoluchowski electrophoretic mobility> is $\mu_e=\epsilon\zeta/\eta$. The sign of $\zeta$ fixes the direction. This transport is different from <dielectrophoresis>, which can move a neutral polarizable particle in a nonuniform field.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Here $r\gg\lambda_D$, so the thin-double-layer result gives
$$
\boxed{\mu_e=\frac{(8.85\times10^{-12})(78.5)(0.045)}{10^{-3}}=3.13\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{m^2\,V^{-1}\,s^{-1}},}
$$
and therefore **$v=3.13\times10^{-5}$ m s$^{-1}$**, along the field for the positive <zeta potential>.

In the opposite limit $r\ll\lambda_D$, the <Hückel electrophoretic mobility> is $\mu_e=2\epsilon\zeta/(3\eta)$, giving **$2.08\times10^{-8}$ m$^2$ V$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$**, two-thirds of the thin-layer value at the same $\zeta$. Both asymptotic expressions are independent of radius at fixed <zeta potential>; the crossover depends on $r/\lambda_D$. These classical approximations neglect double-layer relaxation and strong-potential corrections.

= c
{parent=25}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <amphiphile packing parameter> is $p=v/(a\ell)$: hydrocarbon volume divided by the product of effective head-group area and maximal tail length. For a spherical core of radius $R$, $v/a=R/3$; requiring $R\leq\ell$ gives **spheres for $p\lesssim1/3$**. Cylindrical cores instead have $v/a=R/2$, favouring cylinders or rods for roughly $1/3<p\lesssim1/2$. Lower-curvature bilayer structures are favoured towards $p\simeq1$; intermediate ranges can include flexible or closed bilayer morphologies.

The charged sulfate head groups make the effective area sensitive to electrostatic repulsion, salt and counterion binding. Screening can reduce that area, increasing $p$ and favouring growth from spheres towards elongated <micelles>. These are geometric selection rules, not exact phase boundaries or a claim that every morphology is stable at any concentration.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a dodecyl tail, take $n=12$. The given relations yield $v=0.3502$ nm$^3$ and $\ell=1.672$ nm. The spherical-core condition is $v/(a\ell)\leq1/3$, hence
$$
\boxed{a_{\min}=\frac{3v}{\ell}=0.628\ \mathrm{nm^2}.}
$$
Equality corresponds to a maximally extended tail reaching the core centre. A smaller head-group area would require a spherical radius larger than the available chain length. The estimate assumes all twelve tail carbons contribute to the hydrocarbon core; partial penetration or hydrated head-group geometry would modify it.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At the limiting area, the spherical core radius is $R=\ell=1.672$ nm. Its <aggregation number> is
$$
N_{\rm agg}=\frac{4\pi R^2}{a}=\frac{4\pi R^3}{3v}\simeq55.9.
$$
Above the <critical micelle concentration>, free <monomers> remain approximately at 8 mM, so 42 mM of surfactant units are in aggregates. Thus
$$
\boxed{c_{\rm micelle}\simeq\frac{50-8}{55.9}=0.751\ \mathrm{mM}.}
$$
The concentration is of whole aggregates, not the concentration of surfactant molecules inside them. The estimate neglects <micelle> polydispersity and concentration-dependent growth.

