= Solution
{parent=A}

**A stable charge-stabilized dispersion has an interaction barrier large compared with $k_BT$.** In <DLVO theory>, the total pair potential combines attractive <van der Waals forces> with repulsion from overlapping <electric double layers>. Similar surface charges raise the free energy of overlap; a sufficiently high positive barrier prevents thermally diffusing particles from reaching the deep primary minimum near contact.

Other stabilization mechanisms include <steric stabilization> by solvated surface chains and combined electrosteric protection by charged chains. Such contributions are additional to the simplest DLVO pair potential. Increasing salt shortens the <Debye length>, so double-layer repulsion becomes shorter-ranged and the protective barrier can decrease.

\Image[paper-2-colloidal-potentials.png]
{title=Original schematic DLVO stability, depletion attraction and brush scaling}

= B
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B}

**Primary-minimum aggregation occurs when particles can cross the repulsive barrier and reach near contact.** Its deep attractive well usually makes escape slow on experimental timescales, giving effectively irreversible coagulation. Higher electrolyte concentration screens the repulsion and can eliminate the barrier. The <critical coagulation concentration> is the electrolyte concentration at which rapid, approximately diffusion-limited coagulation sets in under the specified conditions; in an idealized DLVO description it is associated with loss of the protective barrier. Ion valence and surface boundary conditions strongly influence this threshold.

= C
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=C}

**Secondary-minimum flocculation traps particles at a larger separation in a shallow attractive well.** The primary barrier can remain intact, so particles form loose aggregates without reaching molecular contact. Such flocs are often reversible on dilution or a suitable change in ionic strength. If the secondary well is very weak compared with $k_BT$, the dispersion remains largely unassociated; if screening removes the primary barrier, the particles can proceed to the much deeper primary minimum. “Aggregation” and “flocculation” have variable usage, so the physical distinction here is explicitly shallow, separated association versus near-contact coagulation.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <grafting density> is the inverse area per chain:
$$
\boxed{\Sigma\simeq D^{-2}=0.010\ \mathrm{nm^{-2}}=10^{16}\ \mathrm{m^{-2}}.}
$$
For a neutral <polymer brush> in a good solvent, stretching costs $F_{\rm el}/k_BT\sim h^2/(Nb_0^2)$ per chain while excluded-volume interactions cost $F_{\rm int}/k_BT\sim vN^2/(hD^2)$. Minimization gives $h^3\sim vN^3b_0^2/D^2$. With $v\sim b_0^3$,
$$
\boxed{h\sim Nb_0(b_0/D)^{2/3}=6.84\times10^3\ \mathrm{nm}=6.84\ \mu\mathrm m.}
$$
An isolated good-solvent coil has size $\sim b_0N^{3/5}\simeq502$ nm, much larger than $D=10$ nm, consistent with strong overlap. The brush height is below the 20 μm contour length. This estimate assumes good-solvent excluded volume and locally planar geometry; the source gives neither solvent quality nor particle radius, so a unique curved-brush thickness is not established. An ideal-solvent brush has different scaling.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A <depletion force> requires **mobile nonadsorbing <polymers> excluded from the gap**, rather than grafted chains alone. If their depletion layers overlap when the plate separation is $h<2\delta$, the overlap restores accessible solution volume and increases <polymer> <entropy>. For ideal, dilute depletants of bulk osmotic pressure $\Pi$, an idealized interaction free energy per plate area is
$$
\boxed{W(h)\simeq-\Pi(2\delta-h)\quad(0<h<2\delta),\qquad W(h)\simeq0\quad(h\geq2\delta).}
$$
Its attractive pressure is $-\Pi$ in the overlap interval. The layer thickness is of order the ideal-coil <radius of gyration>, $\delta\sim R_g=b_0\sqrt{N/6}$, so **the range scales as $N^{1/2}$**. For the supplied chain parameters, $R_g\simeq81.6$ nm and an order-of-magnitude two-layer range is 163 nm. Grafted brushes instead oppose compression through stretching and osmotic penalties; they can coexist with depletion attraction if free depletants are present, but the mere existence of a grafted layer does not create a reservoir of depleting chains. The source does not specify that reservoir's concentration, so its attraction magnitude cannot be calculated.

