= a
{parent=26}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a <hard-disc> configuration, the potential energy is zero if no particles overlap and infinite otherwise. All allowed configurations therefore have the same configurational Boltzmann factor. At fixed $N,A,T$, their relative free energies differ through the volume of accessible configuration space:
$$
F=U-TS,\qquad U_{\rm configurational}=0,\qquad\boxed{\text{equilibrium maximizes accessible configurational entropy}.}
$$
The kinetic contribution is common to the phases at the same temperature. “Entropy-driven crystallization” does not mean that particles develop an energetic attraction; excluded volume changes the number of possible many-particle arrangements.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At high density, disorder can frustrate efficient local packing and leave particles with very little room to move. An ordered arrangement can give every particle a larger cage of allowed displacements. The extensive gain in vibrational/configurational freedom within these cages can outweigh the loss of distinct spatial arrangements of cages. **Visual order is therefore not a reliable measure of <entropy>.** At low density the fluid's freedom to rearrange dominates, whereas sufficiently dense packing can favour a crystal through accessible free volume.

= b
{parent=26}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Freeze neighbouring particles and approximate the mobile particle's accessible area by $a_c$. Its one-particle translational contribution to the <canonical partition function> is $a_c/\Lambda^2$ in two dimensions. Neglecting correlations between different cages,
$$
\boxed{Z_{\rm cell}\simeq(a_c/\Lambda^2)^N,\qquad F=-Nk_BT\ln(a_c/\Lambda^2).}
$$
There is no additional uncancelled $N!$: assigning particles to cages removes permutations, while restoring all $N!$ assignments cancels the indistinguishability factor. The result is a free-volume approximation; collective cage motion is excluded by construction.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The particle centre can move approximately $\ell-\sigma$ from the cell centre towards each of its four nearest fixed neighbours. Approximating the actual curved boundary by a square gives
$$
\boxed{a_c\simeq[2(\ell-\sigma)]^2=4(\ell-\sigma)^2.}
$$
This is the area accessible to the **centre**, not the geometrical area enclosed by the fixed particle centres or an area computed using $\sigma/2$. Two equal particles must keep their centres at least $\sigma$ apart.

= c
{parent=26}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a square lattice, $A=N\ell^2$. Substituting $\ell=\sqrt{A/N}$ into the preceding result gives
$$
F=-2Nk_BT\ln\left[\frac2\Lambda\frac{\sqrt A-\sigma\sqrt N}{\sqrt N}\right].
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{B_{\square}=N\sigma^2,\qquad C=2/\Lambda.}
$$
Although the requested parameterization emphasizes particle and thermal parameters, $B$ necessarily includes $N$ because it is a total close-packed area. At **$A=B_{\square}$**, neighbours touch, the cell free area vanishes and this classical cell model gives $F\to+\infty$. The two logarithms in the printed form are dimensionful separately but their combined argument is dimensionless. For $A<B_{\square}$ the presumed square lattice is geometrically impossible.

= d
{parent=26}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A triangular lattice has area per particle $\sqrt3\ell_{\rm hex}^2/2$, so $\ell_{\rm hex}=\sqrt{2A/(\sqrt3N)}$. Inserting this into the supplied accessible area yields
$$
\frac{a_c^{\rm hex}}{\Lambda^2}=\left[\frac2\Lambda\frac{\sqrt A-\sigma\sqrt{\sqrt3N/2}}{\sqrt N}\right]^2.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{F_{\rm hex}=-2Nk_BT\ln\left[\frac2\Lambda\frac{\sqrt A-\sqrt{B_{\rm hex}}}{\sqrt N}\right],\qquad B_{\rm hex}=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}N\sigma^2.}
$$
The prefactor $C$ remains $2/\Lambda$. The lattice is triangular in terms of particle centres; “hexagonal” here describes its sixfold coordination and cell symmetry.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Since $B_{\rm hex}<B_{\square}$, the hexagonal lattice has larger accessible cell area at any common allowed total area $A>B_{\square}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{F_{\rm hex}<F_{\square},\qquad S_{\rm hex}-S_{\square}=Nk_B\ln(a_c^{\rm hex}/a_c^{\square})>0.}
$$
The kinetic entropies cancel at the same temperature. **The triangular lattice is favoured and has higher <entropy> within this cell model**, even though both structures look ordered. More efficient packing leaves more local displacement freedom at a fixed number density. This comparison does not by itself establish whether either crystal is stable against the fluid.

= e
{parent=26}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Allow the total area and particles to divide between phases: $A=A_1+A_2$, $N=N_1+N_2$. Minimize $F_1(N_1,A_1,T)+F_2(N_2,A_2,T)$ subject to these constraints. Transferring area requires equal pressure and transferring particles requires equal <chemical potential>:
$$
\boxed{P_1=P_2,\qquad\mu_1=\mu_2.}
$$
Equivalently, take the lower convex envelope of free energy, using a <common-tangent construction for phase coexistence>. The lever rule fixes phase fractions between coexistence densities. Since the hexagonal branch lies below the square branch throughout their common domain here, this particular comparison supplies no stable square–hexagonal coexistence. A separate fluid free energy is needed to predict fluid–ordered coexistence and its endpoints.

For the actual two-dimensional hard-disc system, a <hexatic phase> can intervene between isotropic fluid and crystal; the one-particle cell model cannot describe that orientational-order physics. It is therefore safe to predict competition between fluid and ordered phases, but not a precise two-phase interval or a direct fluid–crystal transition from these two crystal branches alone.

= 27
{parent=Section E}
{scope}

= a
{parent=27}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Assume all contributing decay rates satisfy $\nu_l>0$ and the summed expression is a nonnegative density. Then
$$
\int_0^\infty p(t)\,dt=\sum_lA_l\int_0^\infty\nu_le^{-\nu_lt}\,dt=\sum_lA_l.
$$
Thus **$\sum_lA_l=1$ normalizes the first-passage distribution**. A zero decay rate would require separate treatment of a nonabsorbing component; eventual absorption is implicit here. Individual spectral coefficients need not all be positive, but the full density must be.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For an unnormalized representation, divide the first moment by its total mass. Integration by parts gives
$$
\int_0^\infty t\nu_le^{-\nu_lt}\,dt=\frac1{\nu_l}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathcal T=\frac{\sum_lA_l/\nu_l}{\sum_lA_l}.}
$$
For normalized amplitudes the denominator is one. A mode with small weight can still dominate the mean if its lifetime $1/\nu_l$ is sufficiently large; this distinction matters when interpreting the later graph.

= b
{parent=27}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The observed statistic is the **conditional mean among passages completed before $T=t_{\rm obs}$**, not a mean in which unfinished trajectories are assigned duration $T$. Its normalization is
$$
Z_T=\int_0^Tp(t)\,dt=\sum_lA_l(1-e^{-\nu_lT}).
$$
For each contribution,
$$
\int_0^Tt\nu_le^{-\nu_lt}dt=\left[-te^{-\nu_lt}\right]_0^T+\frac{1-e^{-\nu_lT}}{\nu_l}=\frac{1-e^{-\nu_lT}(1+\nu_lT)}{\nu_l}.
$$
Dividing the weighted sum of these first moments by $Z_T$ gives
$$
\boxed{\mathcal T(T)=\frac{\sum_l(A_l/\nu_l)[1-e^{-\nu_lT}(1+\nu_lT)]}{\sum_lA_l(1-e^{-\nu_lT})}.}
$$
The denominator records the fraction of trajectories actually observed to finish.

= c
{parent=27}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Set $x=\nu_lT$. Expanding $e^{-x}=1-x+x^2/2-x^3/6+\cdots$ gives $1-e^{-x}=x+O(x^2)$ and $1-e^{-x}(1+x)=x^2/2+O(x^3)$. The numerator contribution is therefore $A_l\nu_lT^2/2+O(T^3)$ and the denominator contribution is $A_l\nu_lT+O(T^2)$.

For one mode, cancellation yields
$$
\boxed{\mathcal T(T)=\frac1\nu-\frac{T}{e^{\nu T}-1},\qquad\mathcal T(T)\sim T/2\ (T\to0),\qquad\mathcal T(T)\to1/\nu\ (T\to\infty).}
$$
The curve rises monotonically from zero, initially with slope $1/2$, and rounds towards a horizontal plateau. On logarithmic axes its short-time slope is one. The crossover occurs at observation times of order $1/\nu$.

= d
{parent=27}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

A <disconnectivity graph> joins minima when an energy threshold first permits a path between their basins. Taking thresholds in steps of 2 and admitting transition states with energy **at most** the threshold gives the following merges:

* $-18$: minima 8 and 9, whose exact connecting barrier is $-19$.
* $-14$: minima 6 and 7.
* $-8$: minima 2 and 3.
* $2$: minima 4 and 5, whose exact barrier is 0.25.
* $6$: the 2–3 and 4–5 groups join through the barrier of 5.
* $8$: the 6–7 group joins the 2–3–4–5 group.
* $10$: minimum 1 joins that group.
* $12$: the 8–9 group joins the rest.

**These eight merges connect all nine minima without duplicating a basin.** Minimum endpoints lie at their stated energies; branch heights are the discretized superbasin thresholds, not necessarily the exact saddle energies. If a strictly-less-than threshold convention is used, barriers exactly on a grid line join at the next grid level; the physical connectivity is unchanged.

\Image[paper-2-disconnectivity.png]
{title=Original disconnectivity tree with two-unit superbasin thresholds and single-mode observation-time crossover}

= e
{parent=27}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The initial slope near one is the short-observation regime $\mathcal T(T)\sim T/2$. Successively longer observations include trajectories that enter progressively longer-lived traps before reaching the absorbing minimum. **The four marked shoulders represent successive relaxation timescales**, not four distinct equilibrium phases. A plateau appears when the contributing faster modes have largely completed but the next slow component has not yet substantially changed the conditional mean. The ultimate plateau is the true <mean first-passage time>, approximately $e^{32.3}$ in the plotted time units.

To estimate a rate, locate the rounding timescale at which that mode stops growing with the observation window, using $\nu_lT\sim1$; the final completion of a crossover requires several lifetimes. Graphical transitions lie approximately around $\ln T\sim8$, 23, 29 and 35, giving only order-of-magnitude estimates
$$
\boxed{\nu_l\sim e^{-8},\ e^{-23},\ e^{-29},\ e^{-35}\ \text{in inverse plot-time units}.}
$$
These are roughly $3\times10^{-4}$, $10^{-10}$, $3\times10^{-13}$ and $6\times10^{-16}$, with factors of several uncertainty from reading broad crossovers. Plateau heights are weighted sums $\sum A_l/\nu_l$ divided by completed probability, **not individually $1/\nu_l$**; in particular the final plateau at $\ln\mathcal T\simeq32.3$ does not require the slowest lifetime to be $e^{32.3}$. The table fixes barriers and basin connectivity, but supplies no temperature or attempt-frequency convention that would justify exact rates directly from the energies.

= Section F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= 28
{parent=Section F}
{scope}

= a
{parent=28}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**A hydroxyl-directed <Evans–Saksena reduction> gives the required anti-1,3-diol.** A suitable reagent is tetramethylammonium triacetoxyborohydride in an acidic medium, for example acetic acid/acetonitrile. Ligand exchange between the pre-existing <alcohol> and an acetoxyborohydride group tethers the reducing reagent to the substrate. Intramolecular hydride delivery to the <ketone> then proceeds through a six-membered arrangement, converting C=O to an alkoxide while conserving all existing <stereocentres>. Hydrolysis releases the second hydroxyl group.

The preferred directed transition-state conformation minimizes crowding by the larger carbon-chain substituents. It delivers the new C–H bond on the face that leaves the two hydroxyl groups **anti**, as in B: with the original hydroxyl drawn as a wedge in the printed zigzag, the new hydroxyl is dashed. The intervening methyl <stereocentre> is retained. Choosing an undirected borohydride reduction would not by itself rationalize this strong substrate-controlled relationship.

= b
{parent=28}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Dibutylboron triflate coordinates the imide carbonyl and triethylamine removes the propionyl $\alpha$ proton, giving predominantly the **Z dibutylboron <enolate>**. Its connectivity is $\mathrm{Aux-N-C(O-BBu_2)=CHMe}$; the imide auxiliary remains attached, rather than being displaced during enolization. Coordination of cinnamaldehyde's oxygen to boron organizes a six-membered <Zimmerman–Traxler transition state>.

A chair with the <aldehyde>'s styryl group pseudoequatorial avoids the auxiliary's bulky benzyl side. Formation of the C–C bond and transfer of the <aldehyde> oxygen into the boron coordination sphere give the boron aldolate, which hydrolyses to D. **A Z <enolate> gives the syn aldol relationship.** In the printed product zigzag, the labelled $\alpha$-methyl bond should therefore be a solid wedge, on the same stereochemical face as the neighbouring solid-wedge hydroxyl bond. With the stated connectivity, the labelled carbon is **R**: priority is imide carbonyl, then $\mathrm{CH(OH)CH=CHPh}$, then methyl, then hydrogen. The adjacent hydroxyl centre is S. These assignments refer to the actual auxiliary and product orientation, not an arbitrary choice of an otherwise equivalent chair.

= c
{parent=28}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The free allylic hydroxyl can hydrogen-bond to the peracid and accelerate directed oxygen transfer to the nearby double bond. The remote styryl double bond lacks this directing contact. **Epoxidation of the hydroxyl-adjacent <alkene> is therefore favoured**, even though both <alkenes> are in principle oxidizable. <Peracid epoxidation> is a concerted oxygen transfer: <alkene> $\pi$ donation to terminal peroxide oxygen, O–O cleavage, proton transfer and carbonyl re-formation occur through a cyclic transition-state arrangement. The <alkene>'s E geometry is retained as a trans relationship between its substituents in the <epoxide>.

The two <alkene> faces are diastereotopic because the molecule already contains <stereocentres>. A plausible substrate-controlled conformation minimizes allylic strain and presents the face opposite the bulky OTBS-bearing part of the chain to the oxidant. **Predict <epoxide> oxygen on the face opposite OTBS**, retaining the pre-existing methyl and OTBS configurations. Hydrogen-bond direction, steric approach and conformational population compete; one cannot deduce an exact diastereomeric ratio from the flat drawing alone. The same facial preference has been discussed for this synthetic series.<Recent results toward the stereoselective synthesis of biologically active natural products by Luiz C. Dias and others>[2007, synthesis section and Scheme 2, epoxidation opposite OTBS]{ref}

= d
{parent=28}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The acetylide attacks the less substituted terminal <epoxide> carbon by <SN2> ring opening. The C–O bond at that carbon breaks, leaving the oxygen attached to the original substituted <stereocentre>. Protonation gives $\mathrm{BnOCH_2CH(OH)CH_2C\equiv CMe}$. Dissolving-metal reduction then gives the E <alkene> and also cleaves the benzyl ether.

Thus **H is $\mathrm{HOCH_2CH(OH)CH_2CH=CHMe}$ with an E double bond**, and its original epoxide-derived <stereocentre> is retained geometrically. No new tetrahedral <stereocentre> is formed: the attacked <epoxide> carbon becomes CH$_2$. The new stereochemical information is the **E <alkene> geometry**. Calling its secondary hydroxyl carbon “newly generated” would confuse changing its functional environment with creating a new <stereocentre>.

= e
{parent=28}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The secondary <alcohol> of H condenses with the <aldehyde> under acid catalysis to form a hemiacetal, then loses water to an <oxocarbenium ion>. Its tethered E <alkene> attacks the aldehyde-derived carbon through a chair-like six-membered arrangement: the <alkene> carbon farther from the <alcohol> forms the new C–C bond, placing a carbocation at the nearer <alkene> carbon. Trifluoroacetate captures that cation; the basic methanolic work-up cleaves the trifluoroacetate <ester> to reveal the ring hydroxyl group. This is a <Prins cyclization>.

Number the product ring from oxygen through the aldehyde-derived carbon as C2, then the alkene-derived methyl-bearing carbon C3, the hydroxyl-bearing carbon C4, the intervening methylene C5 and the original <alcohol> carbon C6. A chair keeping the bulky C2 side chain and C6 hydroxymethyl group equatorial predicts **2,6-cis substitution**. Suprafacial <alkene> capture preserves the E-alkene-derived relationship, giving **2,3-trans** between the C2 side chain and C3 methyl. Preferred equatorial trapping of the C4 cation gives **3,4-trans**, hence the **2,4,6-cis arrangement of the substituent-bearing bonds** in the favoured chair. In the printed flat-ring orientation, draw the C2 side chain, C4 hydroxyl and C6 hydroxymethyl behind the plane, and C3 methyl in front. Existing <stereocentres> in the <aldehyde> side chain and at C6 are retained; they anchor the absolute face of this assignment.

\Image[paper-2-stereochemical-products.png]
{title=Original product connectivity and stereochemical models for the directed reductions, aldol and Prins steps}

= 29
{parent=Section F}
{scope}

= a
{parent=29}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

DBU removes the proton $\alpha$ to sulfonium, generating the propargyl-substituted <sulfur ylide> $\mathrm{Me_2S^+-CH^-C\equiv CMe}$. Its carbon attacks the enone $\beta$ carbon bearing indolyl, shifting the conjugated electrons into an <enolate>. The new intermediate has the carbon sequence $\mathrm{O^-{-}C(Ph)=C(CN)-CH(indolyl)-CH(SMe_2^+)(C\equiv CMe)}$.

Intramolecular attack by the <enolate> oxygen on the sulfonium-bearing carbon displaces <dimethyl sulfide> and closes a five-membered ring. **This conjugate addition–O-alkylation sequence gives the dihydrofuran**, retaining the C≡CMe substituent and placing the original carbonyl oxygen in the ring. <Enolate> carbon alkylation would instead give a cyclopropane; the observed oxygen-containing ring identifies the alternative closure. An approach and betaine conformation minimizing crowding between indolyl and alkynyl substituents, followed by stereoinverting displacement at the sulfonium carbon, rationalizes the depicted trans product. The result is racemic in the absence of an external chiral influence.

= b
{parent=29}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The first two operations invert the allylic hydroxyl group. Triphenylphosphine adds to the azodicarboxylate, generating the reagent pair that converts the <alcohol> into an alkoxyphosphonium leaving group. Benzoate displaces it with inversion at carbon, giving the inverted benzoate <ester>. Methoxide then cleaves that <ester> by <nucleophilic acyl substitution>, **retaining the newly inverted carbon–oxygen <stereochemistry>** because cleavage occurs at the acyl group, not by substitution at the stereogenic carbon.

The inverted allylic <alcohol> exchanges with the orthoacetate under acid catalysis. Ethanol loss produces an allyl ketene acetal, schematically $\mathrm{CH_2=C(OEt)-O-(allyl)}$. A six-membered suprafacial <Johnson-Claisen rearrangement> breaks the allylic C–O bond, shifts the ring <alkene> by one position and forms the new bond from the original <alkene> terminus to the ketene-acetal CH$_2$. The result is the $\mathrm{-CH_2CO_2Et}$ substituent at the carbon that was the remote end of the original double bond. A chair-like <transition state> placing the larger substituents away from axial crowding explains the stereochemical transfer; the inverted <alcohol> configuration selects the corresponding face.

Finally, TiCl$_4$ activates the anomeric methoxy group and methoxide departure creates an <oxocarbenium ion>. Hydride transfer from triethylsilane replaces that methoxy substituent by hydrogen. The ring oxygen and pendant OTBS group remain intact. **The sequence is inversion, stereospecific allylic rearrangement, then reductive removal of the anomeric methoxy group**, rather than direct reduction or hydrolysis of the <ester> introduced by the Claisen step.

= c
{parent=29}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**A consistent proposed sequence is desilylation, five-membered hemiketal formation, retro hetero-Diels–Alder opening, intramolecular aldol addition and hemiacetal closure.** The essential carbon rearrangement is loss of the original ketone–side-chain bond and formation of a bond between its two neighbouring carbons; it is not a simple <Michael addition>.

For an explicit atom map, label the starting six-membered ring $A-B=C-D-E-O_r-A$, with the <ketone> at $D$, methyl substituents at $A$ and $B$, the <hydroxy group> at $A$, and the side chain $E-X-T-\mathrm{Et}$. Here $X$ is the methylene and $T$ is the carbon bearing the protected <alcohol>. Let $O_D$ be the original <ketone> oxygen and $O_T$ the protected <alcohol> oxygen. These labels identify atoms, not new substituents.

First, <Fluoride> removes the silyl protecting group from $O_T$. The resulting <alcohol> attacks the protonated <ketone> at $D$, forming the five-membered <hemiketal> $D-E-X-T-O_T-D$. The original oxygen $O_D$ is now a <hydroxy group>; the original pyran ring remains intact at this stage.

Next, The original pyran ring can undergo a <retro hetero-Diels-Alder reaction>. The three electron-pair movements are $B=C\to C=D$, $D-E\to E=O_r$ and $O_r-A\to A=B$: break $D-E$ and $O_r-A$, and form the three indicated <double bonds>. The result is the tethered aldehyde/dienol ester
$$
\mathrm{Me}C_A(OH)=C_B(\mathrm{Me})-CH_C=C_D(OH)-O_T-CH_T(\mathrm{Et})-CH_{2,X}-CH_E=O_r.
$$
The temporary $D-O_T$ bond keeps both reacting ends within the same <molecule>. At $D$, both oxygen substituents remain attached, and the new $D-O_T$ tether prevents separation into independent fragments.

Then The $C=D$ <enol> attacks the tethered aldehyde at $E$. Formation of $C-E$, restoration of $D=O_D$, and proton transfer give the six-membered <lactone> ring $C-D-O_T-T-X-E-C$, with $E-O_rH$. This is an <intramolecular aldol reaction>, and it supplies the crucial new carbon–carbon bond without losing any carbon atom.

Finally, <Keto-enol tautomerism> at $A=B$ gives the methyl <ketone> at $A$ and introduces the hydrogen at $B$. The $E-O_rH$ <alcohol> then attacks that <ketone>, closing the five-membered <hemiacetal> ring $A-B-C-E-O_r-A$. The resulting fused rings have exactly the product's connectivity: $D$ is the <lactone> carbonyl, $O_T$ is its ring oxygen, and $O_r$ is the five-membered-ring oxygen.

The <stereocentre> at $T$ survives throughout this sequence. The configuration initially at $E$ is erased at the aldehyde stage, and those at $A$, $B$, $C$ and $E$ arise during the later cyclization and proton-transfer steps. A folded <intramolecular aldol reaction> followed by reversible <hemiacetal> formation is consistent with the drawn cis ring junction, but connectivity and orbital bookkeeping alone do not prove that it is the unique selected <diastereomer>. In particular, a claim of simple retention at $E$ would contradict this atom map.

A related pyranone-to-fused-<lactone> rearrangement under aqueous <hydrogen fluoride> is experimentally reported; that account also explicitly qualifies the relative <stereochemistry> as assigned on mechanistic grounds. The sequence above is a proposed explanation of the examination structure, rather than a mechanism claimed to have been experimentally established in that paper.<Total synthesis of (±)-tirandamycin A by Philip DeShong and others>[Pages 5220–5221, Schemes II–III and the discussion of pyranones 10 and 13 giving lactone 12]{ref}

